#usda 1.0
(
    defaultPrim = "Motor"
    upAxis = "Y"
    metersPerUnit = 1
)

# A DRIVE MOTOR as a real part: a mass, a housing that collides, a torque curve, an
# efficiency, a power draw and a waste-heat output. One machine carries as many of these
# as it has driven wheels, and each is independently commanded, independently sized and
# independently able to overheat or fail.
#
# HOW A VESSEL COMPOSES IT. One reference arc per motor, named for the wheel it turns,
# with the wheel target and the demand wired on the instance:
#
#   def Xform "Motor_FL" (
#       prepend references = @lunco://components/mobility/motor.usda@</Motor>
#   )
#   {
#       double3 xformOp:translate = (0.8, -0.2, 1.0)
#       uniform token[] xformOpOrder = ["xformOp:translate"]
#
#       # WHICH WHEEL this motor turns. Never a joint: a joint exists only in the
#       # physical drivetrain, a wheel exists in both realizations. Authored
#       # unconditionally, OUTSIDE every variant block.
#       prepend rel lunco:motor:drivenWheel = </Rover/Wheel_FL>
#
#       # WHAT it is told to do: -1..1, from the vessel's command surface (the OBC) or
#       # from a drive law. A parameter is an input with a constant instead of a wire.
#       float inputs:demand.connect = </Rover/OBC.outputs:drive_left>
#   }
#
# `PhysicsDriveAPI` IS NOT THE MOTOR. It is the motor's OUTPUT COUPLING to the joint:
# a multiple-apply API on a CONSTRAINT, carrying a target, a damping and a force cap.
# It cannot hold a mass, an efficiency, a thermal model or a power draw, so it cannot be
# the part. The motor is this prim; it NAMES its WHEEL. Where that wheel has a joint the
# loader follows wheel → joint and writes that joint's
# `drive:angular:physics:targetVelocity` / `:maxForce` from the curve below; where it does
# not, the raycast wheel consumes the same axle torque and no-load speed directly. Keying
# on the wheel is what makes raycast/physical parity structural — the powertrain is
# identical and only the wheel's torque→force step differs.
# MuJoCo draws the same actuator/constraint line: `<actuator>` is its own element.
#
# THE CURVE IS TWO SCALARS. τ(ω) = stallTorque · (1 − ω / noLoadSpeed), clamped at zero —
# the linear DC/BLDC characteristic, and the whole first-order motor. See `LunCoMotorAPI`
# in the schema for why a sampled array was rejected. A motor whose real curve is not
# linear gets a `LunCoProgramAPI` child that computes torque instead.
#
#   inputs:demand ──► τ(ω) curve ──► outputs:torque ──► gearbox ──► driven wheel
#                            └─────► outputs:heat ───► thermal program / radiator
#
# THE SHAFT SIDE AND ELECTRICAL FACET LIVE ON THE SAME PART. A vessel's Electrical
# Scope includes the actual motor path in `CollectionAPI:components`; runtime projection
# compiles all included facets into one circuit DAE, never one solver per part.
def Xform "Motor" (
    doc = "Reusable driven-wheel motor with mass, torque, efficiency, power, and thermal telemetry."
    kind = "component"
    prepend apiSchemas = [
        "LunCoCatalogAPI",
        "PhysicsMassAPI",
        "LunCoMotorAPI",
        "LunCoProgramAPI",
    ]
)
{
    uniform bool lunco:spawnable = true
    uniform token info:implementationSource = "sourceAsset"
    uniform asset info:sourceAsset = @lunco://models/LunCo/Electrical/DCMotor.mo@
    custom token connectors:p

    # --- Physics ---
    # NO `PhysicsRigidBodyAPI`, and this is the whole story of a bug that shipped:
    # this file used to apply it, on the belief that "a motor bolted to a rover is a
    # child of the chassis body and this is inert". It is not inert. The loader's rule
    # (`lunco-usd-avian`) is that an APPLIED `PhysicsRigidBodyAPI` makes that prim its
    # own body — ancestry is not consulted, because nested-body-plus-joint is exactly
    # how the `physical` drivetrain mounts its wheels. So every mounted motor became a
    # free dynamic body that no joint held and no collider stopped, and all four fell
    # out of every rover on the first physics step. Nothing else noticed: the rovers
    # still drove, still steered, still made their top speed, and only a screenshot of
    # motors lying on the regolith gave it away. `scenes/tests/parts_attached.usda`
    # is the gate that now catches it.
    #
    # An internal part is not a body. It has mass and it renders; the chassis it is
    # mounted on is the body, and a part that must move relative to its host says so
    # with a JOINT — the same rule `gearbox.usda` already follows.
    float physics:mass = 8.0 (
        customData = {
            double min = 0.5
            double max = 200
            string unit = "kg"
        }
    )

    # --- Motor characteristic (LunCoMotorAPI) ---
    # SHAFT quantities, before any reduction.
    #
    # A MOTOR IS SIZED TO THE TRACTION IT CAN USE. Torque past the friction limit does
    # not move the vehicle; it spins the wheel. 1.5 N·m here is ~255 N·m at the axle
    # through the 200:1 harmonic drive at 0.85 efficiency.
    #
    # THIS CATALOGUE FIGURE IS A FLOOR, NOT THE FLEET'S TORQUE. Every shipped rocker-bogie
    # tier overrides it (`variants/rover_*.usda`: awful 2.22, medium 3.84, easy 5.28), so
    # the difficulty ladder — not this number — sets what a vehicle actually pulls.
    #
    # ⚠ THE AXLE TORQUE IS WELL ABOVE THE TRACTION LIMIT. For the six-wheel rocker-bogie
    # (532 kg authored: 300 chassis + 2×50 rockers + 2×30 bogies + 6×12 wheels):
    #
    #   τ_traction = μ·N·r = 0.8 × (532 × 1.62 / 6) × 0.4 m ≈ 46 N·m per wheel
    #
    # against 255 N·m here and 653 N·m on `rover_medium`. That ratio does NOT produce
    # permanent wheelspin, because the drive is a VELOCITY SERVO — torque authority above
    # what traction can use is only ever spent reaching the commanded speed, not exceeding
    # it. It does mean acceleration transients are traction-limited rather than
    # torque-limited, which is realistic. Size any NEW motor against the 46 N·m figure.
    #
    # SPEED IS THE PAIR (WINDING, RATIO), AND ONLY THE PAIR. What the vehicle feels is
    # `noLoadSpeed / ratio` = 24 rad/s at the axle and `stallTorque · ratio · efficiency`
    # = 170 N·m; any (winding, ratio) with those two products drives identically —
    # EXCEPT for inertia, which is why the pair is chosen and not just the products.
    #
    # ⚠ THE REDUCTION IS THE INERTIA. Reflected rotor inertia is `J · ratio²`, so the
    # ratio is not a free variable to be traded against the winding: at the 1200:1 this
    # catalogue used to carry, a 1.2e-4 kg·m² rotor reflected to **173 kg·m²** per axle —
    # 87× the wheel's own ½mr² ≈ 2 kg·m², about 1080 kg of apparent mass per wheel on a
    # 532 kg rover. MEASURED consequence: `allocation_spec`'s forward leg covered 9.15 m
    # where it had always covered 13.8 m, and every rover in the fleet was still
    # accelerating after eight seconds.
    #
    # That 1200:1 was chosen while reflected inertia was ORPHANED — computed and never
    # applied — together with a 14 400 rad/s winding (≈137 000 rpm, no real machine) to
    # buy road speed back. When `486ce6b0` made the inertia live, the ratio² term arrived
    # with it and the pair became untenable. Rebalanced to 200:1 with a 2400 rad/s
    # winding: both axle products are unchanged to the digit, so the difficulty ladder,
    # the top speed and every authored tier behave exactly as before, while the reflected
    # inertia falls to 4.8 kg·m² — the same order as the wheel it drives, which is what a
    # geared rover should feel.
    #
    # Size a NEW motor by holding those two products, not by copying a winding: raising
    # the winding to buy speed costs ratio² in inertia unless the reduction comes down
    # with it. The authentic-speed alternative was measured and rejected on usability
    # (Lunokhod's real 2.0 km/h turned the school's 2.7 km round trip into a multi-hour
    # wait) — see `motors/lunokhod.usda`, which keeps that slow axle honestly.
    #
    # The ceiling is not the cruise: drive force rolls off as `1 − v/v_max` while contact
    # drag grows with v, so a rover settles well short of it — against a 9.6 m/s rim
    # ceiling (24 rad/s × 0.4 m). See `rocker_bogie.usda` `Motor_FL` for measured
    # terminal speeds.
    #
    # ── SIZED AGAINST THE FRICTION CONE, NOT AGAINST AMBITION ─────────────────
    #
    # 1.0, down from 1.5 → `1.0 × 200 × 0.85` = 170 N·m at the axle. A wheel slips
    # the moment commanded torque passes `μ·N·r`; on the standard 1100 kg
    # four-wheeler (N ≈ 445 N, r = 0.4) with the regolith tyre's μ = 1.5 that cone
    # is 267 N·m, so 170 leaves ~36% of margin — enough to absorb the load transfer
    # that unloads the front axle under acceleration, which is where the last of
    # the wheelspin lived once μ went up.
    #
    # At 1.5 the axle asked for 255 N·m against that same 267 N·m cone: a margin of
    # 5%, i.e. spin on any launch, any slope and any wheel that momentarily went
    # light. THE TORQUE ABOVE THE CONE WAS NEVER DOING ANYTHING — traction, not the
    # motor, sets what a rover can push — so this costs no acceleration it could
    # actually deliver; it only stops paying for the difference in visible slip.
    #
    # The `variants/rover_*.usda` tiers override this UP (2.22 … 5.28) on purpose:
    # a deliberately over-torqued rover on a poor tyre IS the difficulty exercise.
    # Untouched here.
    float lunco:motor:stallTorque = 1.0
    # FLEET SPEED — A PACING OVERRIDE, KEPT INSIDE WHAT THE TYRE CAN DELIVER.
    #
    # 4800 rad/s through the 200:1 gives a 24 rad/s axle — 9.6 m/s of rim ceiling on
    # the 0.4 m wheel, 2x the 2400 this originally carried (12 rad/s axle, 4.8 m/s
    # rim). The multi-kilometre traverses still want the pacing help — the usability
    # argument `motors/lunokhod.usda` makes against authentic speeds — but only as
    # far as traction can convert it.
    #
    # DOWN FROM 24000 (a 10x, 120 rad/s axle, 48 m/s rim), which asked for far more
    # than the ground can give: the servo targets `throttle · ω_max`, so every
    # throttled wheel sat permanently in the SATURATED branch of
    # `lunco_mobility::update_wheel_spin` — the whole surplus went into ω. That is
    # exactly the "wheels rotate fast but the rover barely moves" report: thrust was
    # pinned at the friction cone μ·N while the tyre burned the rest as slip, so the
    # ceiling bought spin, not travel. A ceiling the tyre can approach is worth more
    # road speed than a ceiling it cannot.
    #
    # ⚠ STILL NOT AUTHENTIC HARDWARE. 4800 rad/s is ~46 000 rpm; peak mechanical
    # power (stall·noLoad/4) is ~1.8 kW, which now at least sits under the 2000 W
    # `inputs:rated_power` below (nothing enforces that rating today — the powertrain
    # reads only the two curve scalars, `usd-sim/powertrain.rs`).
    #
    # ⚠ THE RIM CEILING IS NOT THE ROAD SPEED. `vessels/rovers/rocker_bogie.usda`
    # `Motor_FL` holds the measured table (12 rad/s → 2.71 m/s, 19.5 rad/s →
    # 5.72 m/s) and the standing rule: the response is non-linear and SUPERLINEAR,
    # so measure it, never derive it.
    #
    # ⚠ WATCH FOR TERRAIN TUNNELLING HERE. The physics collider ring streams 15.6 m
    # tiles around each rover (`terrain-surface/collider_ring.rs`, COLLIDER_DEPTH 9).
    # A vehicle actually reaching tens of m/s crosses a tile in a fraction of a second
    # — the regime where a body outruns its ring and drops through the surface. If
    # rovers start falling through terrain, suspect this number first.
    #
    # To revert: 2400.0 = original, 24000.0 = the 10x this replaces. The six instance
    # overrides in `vessels/rovers/rocker_bogie.usda` restate whatever is chosen here.
    #
    # SET AT CONSTANT TORQUE, deliberately. `stallTorque` is untouched, so axle torque
    # and the whole `variants/rover_*.usda` difficulty ladder are exactly as before —
    # this is a smaller-ceilinged motor, not a re-wind. `motors/fast.usda` is the
    # power-conserving alternative (half the torque for twice the speed) and lands on
    # this same 4800; it is a different trade and is left alone, as are the other
    # winding classes, so that taxonomy stays coherent.
    #
    # Torque FALLS at every non-zero speed relative to the 10x (`τ = stall·(1 −
    # ω/noLoad)`), so a wheel that was already spinning loses the torque it was
    # wasting, not torque it was using. Reflected inertia is UNCHANGED: `J · ratio²`,
    # and the ratio did not move.
    float lunco:motor:noLoadSpeed = 4800.0
    # Continuous rating, below stall: what it can hold all day without cooking. 0.9
    # against a 1.5 stall is 60%, the usual margin. Must always stay ≤ `stallTorque`,
    # or the motor is rated to sustain more than it can ever produce.
    float lunco:motor:continuousTorque = 0.9
    float inputs:efficiency = 0.85
    float inputs:rated_power = 2000.0
    # A small rotor, reflected to the axle multiplied by the ratio SQUARED —
    # 1.2e-4 × 200² ≈ 4.8 kg·m², the same order as the wheel's own ½mr² ≈ 2 kg·m².
    # That reflected inertia is a real part of what a geared rover feels, which is why
    # the ratio is chosen against it and not only against torque — see the winding note.
    float lunco:motor:rotorInertia = 0.00012
    float lunco:motor:ratedVoltage = 28.0
    # Which drive instance on the wheel's joint the output couples to, when the wheel has
    # one. The wheel target itself is authored per-instance by the vessel — a component
    # cannot know which wheel it is on.
    uniform token lunco:motor:driveInstance = "angular"

    # --- Ports ---
    # Command in. Unwired it is 0 and the motor sits there, which is what an uncommanded
    # motor does. A drive law, an OBC port, or a script writes it; -1..1, signed.
    float inputs:demand = 0.0

    # Mechanical output — shaft torque, N·m — for whatever consumes it: a gearbox's
    # `inputs:torque`, or a telemetry channel.
    float outputs:torque

    # Waste heat, watts: (1 − efficiency) of the electrical input. This is the port a
    # thermal model or a radiator consumes; nothing in the physics step reads it.
    float outputs:heat

    # --- Telemetry ---
    # Drive command as a RETAINED channel, so "was this motor actually being
    # told to pull?" needs no per-vessel authoring: every rover referencing this
    # component gets it. Authored on the component, not on the six instances in
    # `rocker_bogie.usda`, because a motor is where a motor's instrumentation
    # belongs.
    #
    # ⚠ IT IS `demand`, NOT `torque`, AND THAT IS NOT A PREFERENCE.
    # A channel reads a LIVE port. `inputs:demand` is one: the wire from the
    # chassis's `outputs:drive_*` writes it every tick. `outputs:torque` is
    # declared below but NOTHING WRITES IT — the delivered torque is computed in
    # `lunco_hardware::axle_torque` and handed straight to the joint motor, so a
    # channel pointed at that attribute would have plotted a flat authored zero
    # forever and looked like a dead drivetrain. Publishing the shaft torque
    # needs a port backend on the crate that computes it (the rule in
    # `docs/architecture/telemetry-subsystem.md` §5b: the backend belongs with
    # the component that owns the value); until that exists, this channel states
    # what is true.
    #
    # `Parameter` is a Component — ONE channel per prim — so this names the one
    # quantity worth a mnemonic and a unit. Everything else the model exposes
    # (`heat`, and every INTERNAL Modelica variable) is published wholesale under
    # the `sim.` namespace by `lunco_cosim::telemetry`, which is why there is no
    # temptation here to invent a channel prim per variable.
    #
    # Unit `1`: demand is a signed FRACTION of full command (−1..1), a ratio with
    # no dimension. The browser renders that as a blank unit cell rather than a
    # literal "1".
    uniform bool lunco:telemetry = true
    uniform token lunco:telemetry:port = "demand"
    uniform token lunco:telemetry:name = "motor_demand"
    uniform token lunco:telemetry:unit = "1"

    # --- Housing: RENDERS ONLY, deliberately no collider ---
    #
    # Internal machinery does not collide. A hub motor lives INSIDE the vehicle
    # envelope, so a collider here does not model anything real — it just fights the
    # chassis and the wheel for the same space. Eight of these, interpenetrating,
    # launched the parity rovers at 846 m/s the first time they shipped with
    # `PhysicsCollisionAPI`.
    #
    # What a motor contributes to physics is MASS, via `PhysicsMassAPI` on the part
    # above. Contacts are for things that touch the world.
    #
    # Sized like real hardware rather than like a placeholder: a ~0.09 m radius,
    # 0.14 m long can is what an 8 kg rover hub motor actually looks like, and it fits
    # inboard of a 0.4 m wheel instead of swallowing it.
    def Cylinder "Body"
    {
        double radius = 0.09
        double height = 0.14
        color3f[] primvars:displayColor = [(0.4, 0.4, 0.4)]
    }

    # --- Optional: thermal behaviour ---
    # A motor that does not model its temperature is still a motor. This child is where
    # the state lives when it does: delete the prim and the mechanics are untouched,
    # which is exactly the separability a `LunCoProgramAPI` child is for (a bolted-on
    # program, not the part's own engine).
    #
    # NOT `realtimeSafe`: it drives no force port, only telemetry. `RoverMotorThermal.mo`
    # is a per-SIDE bank model, so a vessel wires the side command into `drive_left` /
    # `drive_right` and reads `temp_left` / `temp_right` back; a per-motor model replaces
    # only this one asset path.
    def Scope "Thermal" (
        prepend apiSchemas = ["LunCoProgramAPI"]
        doc = "First-order lumped heat balance for the motor bank. Pure telemetry — nothing in the sim acts on the temperature."
    )
    {
        uniform asset info:sourceAsset = @lunco://models/RoverMotorThermal.mo@
        uniform bool lunco:program:realtimeSafe = false

        float inputs:drive_left.connect = </Motor.inputs:demand>
        float inputs:drive_right.connect = </Motor.inputs:demand>
    }
}
